  "body": "single",
  "mass": 500,
  "_note": "The four-legged test creature BUILD-ORDER C2's done-condition asks for, and it is in TESTPACK rather than in core on purpose. RIG-SPEC \u00a75 argues that `legged` belongs in the locomotion type list beside `wheeled` and `tracked` because that is what makes a rideable horse a thing a modder writes rather than a thing core has to grow a system for. A quadruped that walks through the same `Walker` as the soldier, out of a non-core pack, with no core change to make it work, is that argument either being true or not. No horse art exists and none is needed: the horse test starts passing long before it.",
  "_kind": "`vehicle` in the `mount` slot as of **C6**, which is exactly what the C2 version of this note promised: *'It changes kind at C6, when there is something to sit on it.'* There is now. `mount`'s own note has said what this asset is since C1 \u2014 *the horse. A mount is a vehicle with a gait \u2014 RIG-SPEC \u00a71 is the whole reason this slot is in the first cut* \u2014 and this is a pack asset filling it with no core change, which is the horse test's actual claim.",
  "_bent_by_rest_angles": "Same method as `core:soldier`, and the same reason: `offset` must be whole modules and a bone pivots half its own length along its own axis, so the skeleton is authored axis-aligned and bent by `rest` angles at build time. The forelegs bend one way and the hind legs the other, which is the whole of the difference between a knee and a hock \u2014 and it costs no field, because `Leg._bend` reads the direction off the pose. Check it with `--rig testpack:horse`: the forelegs should say `(forward, like a knee)` and the hind legs `(backward, like a hock)`.",
